clusion, $T(15{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-75 \\\\ 48 \\\\ 60\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170636/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170636"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-317825/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "e689255b-7099-50d9-bef5-04745b545fac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-317825/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "25de9e1d-20da-5fe5-91da-d9a9f4fb65dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-317825/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer-field/choices ["q-317825/field-1/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-317825/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "0e6c5ed4-38a3-5c21-a785-e012b3b75edf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-317825/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab0ba19-97e1-5561-bd25-ab1c9573e973" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-317825/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-317825/field-2/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-317825/field-3/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708" :answer/id #uuid "e59179f3-ee79-52a2-9dda-b03feab2e0bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-317825/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "b94fb7fb-671b-51b5-bf57-377bb817d938" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-317825/field-3/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708" :answer-field/choices ["q-317825/field-3/845beb42e162b04150b7b438e2c57c85fb9c5dc5bb6c7f2891f90687bf680708"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-317825" :question/id #uuid "cff63153-15b9-5cd1-ac1e-878b39f8b821" :question/math-academy-id "q-317825" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,$, find  $T(9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2}).$\n\n| $\\,T(9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, since ${v}_{1}+{v}_{2}=0,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2} & =15{v}_{1}-6{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2} \\\\  & =15({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})-6{v}_{1} \\\\  & =15(0)-6{v}_{1} \\\\  & =-6{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nT(9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2})=T(-6{v}_{1}).\n$$\n\nNow, since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that $T(kv)=kT(v).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-6{v}_{1}) & =-6T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =-6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $T(9{v}_{1}+15{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-317825/field-1" "q-317825/field-2" "q-317825/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-317825"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170647/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "1121b90b-2e4c-58b5-9f03-75d06010f56f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170647/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "61827c17-50a8-5b23-8021-20010be82ff0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170647/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "c4a33b6a-544a-5387-ba54-82f9bba5ff59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170647/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "b8ce4da4-0c48-5579-9b09-cf4d16e74f14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170647/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1a5a644c-0a2b-5289-8348-5ee4bcb18dda" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170647/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "81bda7b5-fa33-58d6-95af-ed4090bbbee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170647/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-170647/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-170647/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-170647/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-170647/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-170647/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170647" :question/id #uuid "65a35228-3fa4-5ae7-b7c4-4220ad1c7c6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-170647" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ k\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={k}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-170647/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170647"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-8049/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "9a7098a4-cf8a-556d-8bd3-b993af2a5d5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8049/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "e653c9e1-3da4-5845-ba53-cd0105a93c5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8049/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "7a1201de-428e-5cfb-a53e-7f15f4aa2af2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8049/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a475d07e-edde-51ec-86e0-bfecd24bc2ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8049/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "30236e9b-6cef-5ff8-935d-31ffcb1d64c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-8049/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "70cf97b8-ea31-51f0-92e7-4005557b8960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-8049/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-8049/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-8049/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-8049/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-8049/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-8049/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-8049" :question/id #uuid "72a3fd90-5133-5a81-a0e8-33ac74b2d520" :question/math-academy-id "q-8049" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-8049/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-8049"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5428"] :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation and $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ then find $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$ Noting that $[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T([\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]) & =T(6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=6T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5429"] :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ If $[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(kv+w)=T(kv)+T(w)=kT(v)+T(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(4{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) & =4T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] & =4T({v}_{1})+[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 4T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 4T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5430"] :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}.$ If $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}$ and $[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0},$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} & =5{v}_{1}+12{v}_{1}+12{v}_{2} \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}+12({v}_{1}+{v}_{2}) \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}+12(0) \\\\  & =5{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(5\\mathbf{v}_1).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that $\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}).$ So \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(5{v}_{1}) & =5T({v}_{1}) \\\\  & =5[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $[\\begin{aligned}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5427"] :question/problem "Are the following transformations linear?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_1.$ Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ (kx)(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ {k}^{2}xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}k{x}^{2} \\\\ kxy\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne k{T}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_1$ is not a linear transformation.\n\nNow, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_2.$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3kx \\\\ kx+2ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(3x) \\\\ k(x+2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3x \\\\ x+2y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\ ({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+2{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+{T}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
